open_blocking.evidence:
Every out-of-domain JIT test varies only `hi`. `a_count_outside_the_domain_is_nan_and_never_hangs` (:238) goes through `sum_of_x_plus_k` (:176), whose `lo` is `zero()`, a literal. Every test that has a value `lo` keeps it inside the domain: `a_value_lo_starts_the_fold` (3), `a_pair_that_runs_backwards_is_the_identity` (5 and 4), `an_inner_fold_starting_at_the_outer_index` (i < 8), `a_mask_and_a_number_is_a_count_on_every_tier` (3 and 0). The guard.rs unit tests build `[0, u)`, `[m, n)` and `[u, u)`, and never `[u, literal)`.

I ran a mutation in a scratch copy (/home/user/scratch/a4-r4-mut; diff in .wip/a4/run/r4/mutation.diff.txt). It gives a lone value `lo` the domain `Lit::Mask(true)` instead of `in_domain(lo)`. Every tree test still passes:
- pixelflow-codegen `--lib` (102) and `--test value_bounds_jit` (25) under PIXELFLOW_ISA=avx2 and =avx512;
- all of pixelflow-ir (309 + 75 + 9 + ...).

No other crate's tests compile a value-bounded fold. Under the mutation, `over_values(SUM, &count(lo), &constant(9), X + k)` gives:
- lo = -inf: HANG on both tiers (no answer in 10 s; -inf + 1 never reaches 9);
- lo = NaN, +inf, 2^24+2: 0;
- lo = -1: 45;
- lo = 2.5: 45.5;
- want: NaN in every case (.wip/a4/run/r4/mutation-run.log).

At HEAD the guard is right: probe p8 in .wip/a4/run/r4/rt-probe.rs.txt runs lo in {0, -0.0, 1, 7, 2^24, NaN, +-inf, -1, 2.5, 2^24+2} inside a literal loop and passes on both tiers (rt-probes.log). So this is a gap in the gate, not a wrong value today. On NEON an unguarded NaN `lo` would also hang, because FCMGE is ordered.
